%T A402384 22345679999,223456888888,2234568888899,22345688889999,
%U A402384 223456888999999,2234568899999999,22345689999999999,223456999999999999,2234577777777777777,22345777777777777788,223457777777777777899,2234577777777777779999
%N A402384 a(n) is the smallest positive n-digit number from which no earlier term can be obtained by deleting digits and rearranging the rest.
%C A402384 An n-digit number is a positive integer with exactly n decimal digits (no leading zero); 0 is not a term. A number u can be obtained from m by deleting digits and rearranging the rest if some digits of m, in any positions, can be deleted and the remaining digits put in some order (possibly the order they are in) that spells u. For u shorter than m (the only case that the rule needs), u can be obtained from m if and only if every digit occurs in m at least as often as in u; for example, 20 can be obtained from 2203, but 222 cannot be obtained from 2233, which has only two 2's. The sequence stops at the first n for which an earlier term can be obtained from every n-digit number; this then also holds for every larger n (proved in the notes file).
%C A402384 A402383 is defined by the same rule with "largest" in place of "smallest", and the two sequences correspond term by term: a(n) is obtained from A402383(n) by a substitution of digits (see the Formula and Example sections). Hence this sequence is finite and has exactly as many terms as A402383, namely A(10,0) - 2, where A(m,n) is the Ackermann function: A(0,n) = n+1, A(m+1,0) = A(m,1) and A(m+1,n+1) = A(m,A(m+1,n)), so that A(n,0) = 1, 2, 3, 5, 13, 65533 for n = 0, ..., 5 (A126333). In base b >= 2 the same rule gives exactly A(b,0) - 2 terms. The correspondence and the numbers of terms are proved in the notes file.
%C A402384 Only the number c_d of copies of each digit d matters: u can be obtained from a longer number m exactly when the monomial X_0^(c_0)*...*X_9^(c_9) of u divides that of m. So the terms become monomials of degrees 1, 2, 3, ..., none divisible by an earlier one, and the rule of A402383 becomes the greedy construction of Moreno Socías for monomials of degrees 1, 2, 3, ... (1992, Section 4) without its last monomial (see A402383). The notes file derives the properties of the present sequence from that construction.
%C A402384 Like A402383, this sequence has the greatest possible length among all lists y_1, y_2, ..., y_k of positive integers in which y_i has i digits and, for i < j, y_i cannot be obtained from y_j by deleting digits and rearranging the rest (proved in the notes file from a published bound; see A402383). The word "positive" in the name matters: if 0 were admitted as a 1-digit number, A402383 would not change, but this sequence would become 0, 11, 122, 1233, 12344, 123455, ... and would have one more term (proved in the notes file).
%C A402384 With "deleting digits" alone (the remaining digits keep their order) and "largest", the rule gives A402229. With "deleting digits" alone and "smallest", the rule gives the same first ten terms as the present sequence and then 22345679989 < a(11). The formulation in digits and the correspondence with A402383 were not found in the OEIS or in the literature consulted. See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
%H A402384 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402384/b402384.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..500</a>
%H A402384 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402383/a402383.txt">Notes, proofs and sources</a> (for A402383 and this sequence).
%H A402384 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A402383/a402383_1.txt">Rust program</a> (prints either sequence).
%H A402384 Guillermo Moreno Socías, <a href="https://doi.org/10.7146/math.scand.a-12421">Length of Polynomial Ascending Chains and Primitive Recursiveness</a>, Math. Scand. 71 (1992), 181-205; see Section 4.
%F A402384 Let N = A(10,0) - 2 be the number of terms, with A as in the Comments section. All statements of this section are proved in the notes file.
%F A402384 a(n) is obtained from A402383(n) as follows: let q be the first digit of A402383(n); replace this first digit by 10-q, each later digit equal to q by 0, and each digit e different from q by 9-e, without reordering. Conversely, with d the first digit of a(n), A402383(n) is obtained from a(n) by replacing the first digit by 10-d, each digit 0 by 10-d, and each remaining digit e by 9-e.
%F A402384 The digits of a(n) stand in this order: one copy of its smallest nonzero digit d (the first digit), then all its 0's, then its remaining digits (further copies of d among them) in nondecreasing order.
%F A402384 After a(1) = 1, the first digit of a(n) is 2, 3, ..., 9 in successive runs of consecutive indices. The first term of the run of the digit d is d followed by 0's only (a(2) = 20 for d = 2), and its last term is d followed by 9's only. The first digit is 2 exactly for 2 <= n <= A(9,0) (in base 5: for 2 <= n <= 13 = A(4,0)).
%F A402384 a(N) = 10^N - 1; in base b >= 2 the last term consists of A(b,0) - 2 digits b-1.
%e A402384 a(1) = 1, the smallest positive 1-digit number. a(2) = 20: each of 10, 11, ..., 19 contains a 1, so 1 could be obtained from it, while 20 has no 1.
%e A402384 a(3) = 222: the 3-digit numbers below 222 without a digit 1 are 200, 202, 203, ..., 209 and 220; each of them has a 2 and a 0, so 20 could be obtained from it.
%e A402384 a(4) = 2233: a 4-digit number below 3000 from which no earlier term can be obtained begins with 2, has no 1 and no 0, and has at most two 2's (otherwise 222 could be obtained from it); the smallest such number is 2233.
%e A402384 The substitution of the Formula section turns A402383(4) = 8766 into 2233 (8 -> 2, 7 -> 2, 6 -> 3, 6 -> 3), A402383(11) = 87654320000 into 22345679999 (8 -> 2; 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2 -> 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7; each 0 -> 9), and A402383(2) = 88 into 20 (the first 8 -> 2, the other 8 -> 0). The first twelve terms of the two sequences:
%e A402384    n  A402383(n)    a(n)
%e A402384    1  9             1
%e A402384    2  88            20
%e A402384    3  877           222
%e A402384    4  8766          2233
%e A402384    5  87655         22344
%e A402384    6  876544        223455
%e A402384    7  8765433       2234566
%e A402384    8  87654322      22345677
%e A402384    9  876543211     223456788
%e A402384   10  8765432100    2234567899
%e A402384   11  87654320000   22345679999
%e A402384   12  876543111111  223456888888
%e A402384 In base 3 the same rule gives 1, 20, 222 (written in base 3) and stops: a 4-digit base-3 number has a 1, or a 2 and a 0, or is 2222, so that 1, 20 or 222 can be obtained from it.
%e A402384 In base 4 it gives the 11 terms 1, 20, 222, 2233, 23333, 300000, 3000033, 30003333, 300333333, 3033333333, 33333333333 (written in base 4).
%e A402384 In base 5 it gives 65531 terms; their first digit is 1 for n = 1, 2 for 2 <= n <= 13, 3 for 14 <= n <= 32765 and 4 for 32766 <= n <= 65531, and the last term consists of 65531 digits 4 (proved in the notes file).
%o A402384 (Python)
%o A402384 # Definition-based search, partitioned by the smallest nonzero digit.
%o A402384 B = [([0]*d+[1]+[0]*(9-d), [500]+[0]*(d-1)+[500]*(10-d), d) for d in range(1,10)]
%o A402384 for n in range(1, 23):
%o A402384     candidates = []
%o A402384     for lo, hi, d in B:
%o A402384         if sum(lo) <= n <= sum(hi):
%o A402384             v, left = lo[:], n-sum(lo)
%o A402384             for i in [0]+list(range(d,10)):
%o A402384                 t = min(left, hi[i]-v[i]); v[i] += t; left -= t
%o A402384             candidates.append(([-d, v[0]]+v[d:], v, d))
%o A402384     if not candidates: break
%o A402384     _, v, d = max(candidates)
%o A402384     print(str(d)+'0'*v[0]+str(d)*(v[d]-1)+''.join(str(i)*v[i] for i in range(d+1,10)))
%o A402384     C = []
%o A402384     for lo, hi, d in B:
%o A402384         if any(hi[i] < v[i] for i in range(10)): C.append((lo, hi, d)); continue
%o A402384         lo = lo[:]
%o A402384         for i in range(10):
%o A402384             if lo[i] < v[i]:
%o A402384                 h = hi[:]; h[i] = v[i]-1; C.append((lo[:], h, d)); lo[i] = v[i]
%o A402384     B = [(lo, hi, d) for lo, hi, d in C if sum(hi) > n]
